  'python-search-and-sort': {
    learningPoints: ['How linear and binary search narrow a problem.', 'How sorting reorganizes data for later work.', 'How time and memory costs shape an algorithm choice.'],
    sections: [{ heading: 'Search with a clear strategy', paragraphs: ['Linear search checks items one at a time. It works on any sequence, but the work grows with the number of items.', 'Binary search is faster when the data is sorted: check the middle, discard the half that cannot contain the target, and repeat.'] }, { heading: 'Sort to make the next question easier', paragraphs: ['Sorting changes the order of data so later operations can use that structure. A simple sorting algorithm repeatedly compares neighboring values and swaps them when they are out of order.', 'The right algorithm depends on input size, whether data is already partly sorted, memory limits, and whether the original order matters.'] }, { heading: 'Recognize the reusable patterns', paragraphs: ['Two pointers move from the left and right, while a sliding window maintains a changing range. Hash maps and sets make frequency and membership checks fast.', 'Learn these patterns after basic search and sort. Then use heaps for priority work, shortest-path algorithms for weighted routes, and dynamic programming when subproblems repeat.'] }],
    callouts: [{ title: 'Search space', text: 'The set of possible items an algorithm still needs to consider.', tone: 'teal' }, { title: 'Complexity', text: 'How time or memory grows as the input becomes larger.', tone: 'gold' }],
    examples: [{ title: 'Watch binary search narrow the space', explanation: 'Each step checks the middle value and removes every option that cannot contain the target.', kind: 'diagram', content: 'values = [2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14]\ntarget = 10\n\ncheck 8 -> keep the right half\ncheck 12 -> keep the left half\ncheck 10 -> found', visual: { type: 'algorithm-search', caption: 'Binary search trades a sorted data requirement for fewer checks.', description: 'A step-by-step binary search that narrows a sorted list toward its target.' } }],
    deeper: 'Linear search is O(n) time. Binary search is O(log n) time on sorted random-access data. Comparison sorting commonly ranges from O(n log n) average time to O(n²) for simple methods, with memory and stability depending on the implementation. A practical sequence is data structures, linear/binary search, basic sorting, two pointers, sliding window, recursion, BFS/DFS, heaps, shortest paths, backtracking, dynamic programming, and greedy reasoning.',
    takeaway: 'Good algorithms make the next decision cheaper by using structure, reducing possibilities, or reusing work.',
  },
  'python-recursion-and-graphs': {
    learningPoints: ['How recursion reduces a problem to a smaller version.', 'How stacks and queues control traversal order.', 'How breadth-first and depth-first search explore graphs.'],
    sections: [{ heading: 'Recursion needs a base case', paragraphs: ['A recursive function calls itself with a smaller problem. The base case stops the calls; without it, the function continues until the program fails.', 'Trace one small input by hand so you can see the calls grow and return.'] }, { heading: 'Graphs are relationships', paragraphs: ['A graph stores nodes and the connections between them. Breadth-first search uses a queue to explore nearby nodes first. Depth-first search uses a stack or recursion to follow one path before backing up.', 'Track visited nodes so cycles do not send the algorithm around forever.'] }],
    callouts: [{ title: 'Base case', text: 'The smallest case that can be answered without another recursive call.', tone: 'teal' }, { title: 'Visited set', text: 'A record of nodes already explored so a traversal stays finite.', tone: 'gold' }],
    examples: [{ title: 'Watch a graph traversal spread', explanation: 'The queue holds the next nodes to visit while the visited set prevents repeated work.', kind: 'diagram', content: 'A -> B, C\nB -> D\nC -> D\nD -> end\n\nBFS: A -> B -> C -> D', visual: { type: 'algorithm-sort', caption: 'A graph algorithm turns relationships into an ordered sequence of work.', description: 'A step-by-step breadth-first traversal across connected graph nodes.' } }],
    deeper: 'Depth-first search uses O(V + E) time with an adjacency-list graph, where V is the number of vertices and E is the number of edges. Breadth-first search also uses O(V + E) time and can find shortest paths in unweighted graphs. Trees are graphs with hierarchy; heaps support priority queues; Dijkstra handles non-negative weighted paths; backtracking explores and undoes choices; dynamic programming reuses overlapping subproblems; greedy algorithms commit to locally best choices when the problem supports that strategy.',
    takeaway: 'Recursion, stacks, queues, and graphs are different ways to control the order in which a problem is explored.',
  },
